 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Inference speed is comparable to a deterministic INR (single forward pass) [S1, unofficial]. Training is 2–3× slower than deterministic INRs due to the additional loss computations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Storage cost is comparable to a single SIREN, significantly lower than Deep Ensembles which require 10× the storage [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On volumes up to $512^3$, REV-INR achieved the highest uncertainty-error correlation among three uncertainty-aware INR variants, with correlation coefficients of 0.37–0.85 across six datasets [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Single model inference, closed-form uncertainty, high correlation between uncertainty and error, low storage cost compared to ensembles.
-*   **Limitations:** Slower training time, requires careful handling of NIG parameters (e.g., $\alpha > 1$).
-*   **When to use:** When low-latency inference with uncertainty estimates is required, and storage is constrained.
-*   **When not to use:** When training time is a critical bottleneck and uncertainty estimates are not needed.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Inference is a single forward pass, comparable to a deterministic INR [S1, unofficial]. Training takes 2–3× longer than deterministic INRs due to evidential loss computation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Storage cost is significantly lower than deep ensembles (which require 10× the storage for 10 models) [S1, unofficial]. Exact multiplier relative to deterministic INR is not given, but it is a single model [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On volumes up to $512^3$, REV-INR achieved the highest uncertainty-error correlation among three uncertainty-aware INR variants, with correlation coefficients ranging from 0.37 to 0.85 across six datasets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** High correlation between predicted uncertainty and actual reconstruction error; closed-form uncertainty estimation; inference speed comparable to deterministic models; lower storage than ensembles.
+*   **Limitations:** Slower training time; uncertainty correlation is not perfect (up to 0.85, not 1.0).
+*   **When to use:** When uncertainty quantification is required for scientific volume visualization and storage/compute constraints prevent the use of deep ensembles, but training time is not the primary bottleneck.
+*   **When not to use:** When real-time training is required or when the highest possible reconstruction accuracy (PSNR) is prioritized over uncertainty calibration (other methods like RMDSRN may offer higher PSNR [S1, unofficial]).
 
 ## Background
-REV-INR was proposed by Saklani et al. [120] as an improvement over Deep Ensembles and Monte Carlo Dropout for INR volume visualization [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the high storage cost (10×) of ensembles while maintaining high uncertainty-error correlation [S1, unofficial]. The method is part of the broader field of uncertainty-aware neural representations for scientific volume data [S1, unofficial].
